content:
We need to construct a valid JSON route. The earlier attempt had nested children incorrectly placed. Need proper hierarchy: root mol with children reactions. For each reaction, children are precursor mols (type mol). If a precursor itself is further broken down, that precursor mol should have its own children (reaction). So structure:

{
 "type":"mol",
 "smiles":"COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1",
 "children":[
   {
     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":1914393,
     "children":[
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1"},
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1",
         "children":[
            {
              "type":"reaction",
              "template_id":1914394,
              "children":[
                 {"type":"mol","smiles":"ClCCl"},
                 {"type":"mol","smiles":"FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1"}
              ]
            }
         ]
        }
     ]
   },
   {
     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":1914395,
     "children":[
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1"}
     ]
   }
 ]
}

But note: The root mol is target; the first reaction (1914393) should produce two precursors: the same target? Actually apply_template gave precursors: ['COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1', 'FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1'] So the first precursor is the same as target? That seems odd; maybe the template is a transformation that modifies something else? But we follow given data.

Thus route includes both reactions as separate children of root? Usually each reaction node is child of molecule node, representing a disconnection. The root mol children list can have multiple reaction nodes (alternative routes). So we have two reactions: one using template 1914393, another using 1914395? Actually 1914395 applied to target gave precursor ['OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1'] (single precursor). So that's another disconnection.

Thus final JSON as above.

Now verify route.
